The deepest project method in the stack trace is:

- `com.google.javascript.rhino.jstype.FunctionType.getInstanceType()`

And the immediate caller that triggers it is:

- `com.google.javascript.jscomp.TypeCheck.visitNew(...)`

### Static diagnosis

`TypeCheck.visitNew` currently does this:

```java
JSType type = getJSType(constructor).restrictByNotNullOrUndefined();
if (type.isConstructor() || type.isEmptyType() || type.isUnknownType()) {
  FunctionType fnType = type.toMaybeFunctionType();
  if (fnType != null) {
    visitParameterList(t, n, fnType);
    ensureTyped(t, n, fnType.getInstanceType());
  } else {
    ensureTyped(t, n);
  }
}
```

The problem is that this logic assumes that if `type.isConstructor()` is true, then `fnType.getInstanceType()` is always valid.

But `FunctionType.getInstanceType()` has this precondition:

```java
Preconditions.checkState(hasInstanceType());
```

and:

```java
public boolean hasInstanceType() {
  return isConstructor() || isInterface();
}
```

So `getInstanceType()` is only legal for function types that are actually constructors or interfaces.

### Why this fails on the test case

The test compares two constructors/interfaces:

```js
if (A === B) {
  new B();
}
```

That equality test can cause type narrowing/merging so that the type seen for `B` at `new B()` is no longer a plain constructor function type. It is still treated by `visitNew` as acceptable because `type.isConstructor()` on the outer `JSType` passes, and `type.toMaybeFunctionType()` returns a `FunctionType`. But that resulting `FunctionType` does not necessarily itself satisfy `hasInstanceType()`. Then `fnType.getInstanceType()` throws `IllegalStateException`.

### Root cause hypothesis

`TypeCheck.visitNew(...)` is buggy because it calls `fnType.getInstanceType()` without first checking `fnType.hasInstanceType()`.

This is exactly consistent with the stack trace:
- failure at `FunctionType.getInstanceType`
- called from `TypeCheck.visitNew`

### Most likely buggy method

`com.google.javascript.jscomp.TypeCheck.visitNew(com.google.javascript.jscomp.NodeTraversal, com.google.javascript.rhino.Node)`

### Secondary note

The deeper throwing method (`FunctionType.getInstanceType()`) is behaving as designed: it enforces an invariant. The caller is the one making an invalid assumption.

